  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Horti development Iraq</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The protected horticulture sector in Sulaymaniyah Governorate, located in the Kurdistan Region of Iraq, has experienced significant expansion over the past 15 years. Starting with no greenhouses in 2007, the Bazian valley now hosts an estimated 12,000 to 14,000 plastic tunnel houses (2024), primarily driven by the demand for cucumbers in Federal Iraq. This development has generated employment, and opportunities to substitute imports products.Despite the growth in protected structures, the value chain beyond the farm gate faces several challenges. Issues such as high price volatility, lack of proper grading, packaging, transportation, cool chain logistics, distrust in food safety and in reliability of partnerships; these issues need attention. For successful value chains it is also important that farmers and other value chain stakeholders collaborate with each other.To address these challenges the purpose of this project is to improve cooperation, efficiency, profitability and sustainability along the vegetable (tomato and cucumber) value chain (from end-market to farm). At least 50 actors will be connected, trained and coached in value chain thinking and will be made familiar with post-harvest technologies (grading, sorting, packing), enabling them to adopt best practices and improve their competitiveness.</narrative>
  </description>
